==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|Brittannia|Brittania|Britania}}
* {{alt|la|Bretannia|Brettannia|Brettania}}
* {{alt|la|Brictania|Brintania||Late Latin}}, {{alt|la|Brytannia||Medieval Latin}}

===Etymology===
{{slim-wikipedia|Britain (name)|lang=en}}
{{slim-wikipedia|Britannia (discretiva)|Britannia|lang=la}}
Attested from the 1st century {{BCE}}, directly or from {{bor|la|grc|Βρεττανία}}, {{m|grc|Πρεττανία}} (in {{w|Diodorus}}), earlier {{m|grc|[[νῆσος]] [[Πρεττανική]]}} or {{m|grc|Βρεττανίαι}}, used by {{w|Pytheas}} (4th century {{BCE}}) of the entire archipelago now known as the [[British Isles]].

The Ancient Greek name is ultimately from a {{der|la|cel|-}} ethnonym, reconstructed as early {{der|la|cel-bry|-}} {{m|cel-pro|*Pritani}}, perhaps from a {{der|la|cel-pro|*Kʷritanī}}, {{m|cel-pro|*Kʷritenī}}, whence {{cog|cy|Prydyn||Picts}}, {{cog|sga|Cruthne}}, {{m|sga|Cru(i)then-túath||Picts}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*kʷer-||to do}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}
* Note: only found in hexameters with a short first syllable, except for a single instance of /britt-/ in Lucretius. [[w:ca:Sergi_(gramàtic)|Sergius]] on Donatus testifies to /a:n/ rather than /ann/. Romance descendants seem to consistently point to /tt/.

===Proper noun===
{{la-proper noun|Britannia<1.loc.both>}}

# [[Britain]], i.e. the country of the [[Briton]]s
# [[Great Britain]]
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} [[Brittany]]
#: {{syn|la|Armorica}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} [[Wales]]
#: {{syn|la|Cambria|Wallia}}

====Usage notes====
* {{s|Wales}} In medieval Welsh sources before the 13th century, ''Britannia'' was used both in an expansive sense to refer to the island of Britain and in a restricted sense to refer to Wales, i.e. the remaining land of the Britons. From the 12th century the term was increasingly displaced by {{m|la|Wallia}}, a name derived from Old English, and later by {{m|la|Cambria}}.

